hree paragraphs  that the syndicate amounted to an  invisible government a hidden hand and a  secret power and he said in the same  time that behind our city and state  governments there is an unseen power  which controls them now coerced  prostitution did really exist it did  really exist but it was neither as  prevalent nor as organized as that kind  of wild rhetoric suggested but far from  being consigned to a marginal movement  as hofstetter would have suggested the  scare led to a major piece of national  legislation the Mann Act of nineteen ten  and it gave the first major boost in  power to the agency that would later be  known as the Federal Bureau of  Investigation within a decade the bureau  would be extending its purview from  alleged conspiracies of pimps to alleged  conspiracies of communists getting  another boost in power in the process  and after he took over Jagger Hoover was  very  good at playing that game you know now  he's going after the far right now is  going after the far left always finding  ways to turn whatever the paranoid  moment was into a way of expanding his  bureaucracies power but stories like  that are missing from Richard Hofstadter  famous essay the paranoid style drew  almost all of his examples all but one  and it's sort of a peculiar exception  for movements that were opposed to the  quote unquote right thinking people that  Stanley Cohen mentioned in his  definition of moral panic as Manning the  barricades against the folk Devils so  the result in hofstetter was a distorted  picture in which the country's outsiders  are possessed by fear and its  establishment usually is not there as  they had room for example for a what's  the quote greenback and populist writers  who constructed a great conspiracy of  international bankers but the essay said  nothing about the elites of the era who  perceived populism as a consumer product  of a conspiracy I'll give you a more  recent example of that kind of mirroring  this is this comes way after hofstetter  but involves people who quote hofstetter  a lot in June of 2009 you may remember  an 88 year old neo-nazi named James  winter curve on brun entered the  Holocaust Museum in Washington DC and he  opened fire and killed a security guard  that came 10 days after the murder of  the abortion doctor george tiller in  kansas and it to crimes wind up getting  linked in this kind of free associative  way into a pattern of rising right-wing  violence and some pundits tried to tie  the slayings to a smattering of other  crimes many dating back to the bush  administration which were linked only by  the fact that the criminals all hailed  from one corner or another of the  radical right the paranoid right as it's  often called but the critics of the  paranoid right turned out to be pretty  paranoid too having established this  crime wave to their satisfaction the  analysts quickly moved on to denouncing  the unindicted instigators they blamed  for it bonnie erbe of US News and World  Report pinned the museum's guard on  promoters of hate and said the shooting  was a clarion call for banning hate  speech in the New York Times Bob  herbert wrote that he can't help  thinking that the crimes were just the  beginning and that worst is to come and  if this was thanks in part to the  over-the-top rhetoric of the National  Rifle administration Association Paul  Krugman warned that right-wing extremism  is being systematically fed by the  conservative media and political  establishment the narrative was clear  the right-wing media was responsible for  right-wing violence and right-wing  violence was on the rise so with that  story in mind let me show you a chart  this is from a paper that was published  earlier this year by the combating  terrorism Center at West Point it counts  all the violent right-wing incidents  known to have happened in the u.s. each  year we're talking everything from  vandalism to the Oklahoma City bombing  the first thing you'll probably notice  is that this shaded park over on the  right hand side is a lot taller than the  shaded part on the left hand side and a  lot of the media reaction to the paper  focused on that though if they look  closer they would have seen that the  author of the paper actually mentioned  that that has a lot to do with the fact  that there's a lot better data now they  only started asking the police agencies  to start keeping track of hate crimes in  the like early on but if it's tricky for  I want what I want to actually focus on  here is 2009 when all those voices in  the media we're talking about rising  right-wing violence and we see it going  down and in 2010 remember there was too  few over a health care bill and the  warnings that tea partiers were going to  turn into storm troopers and it keeps  going actually down this is like the the  most interesting rise in violence I've  ever seen I'm finally in 2011 there's an  uptick just like you have upticks in  other years before national elections  the author of the paper thinks that  might be a pattern that happens around  election time maybe you'll notice 1996  it doesn't happen he says made us  because nobody really cared who won in  96 which might be true Bob Dole was  running I don't think he cared but at  any rate my point isn't to theorize  about what explains these zigs and zags  it's just to show you that this downward  slope happened in the exact two years  when everyone was telling us to expect  an upward slope it was  the story that we heard was a remarkable  reversal of the reality on the ground it  was paranoid and as anyone who's ever  read a comment thread about you know the  Koch brothers on a liberal blog can tell  you it's the kind of paranoia that can  frequently turn into conspiracy theories  I wrote this book it's got two big  themes first one I mentioned earlier  it's that conspiracy theories aren't  just a fringe thing the people in power  can be paranoid too and the others that  we can learn a lot from conspiracy  theories even when they are not accurate  some conspiracy theories are accurate of  course I got a chapter in here about the  revelations of the 1970s when all sorts  of ugly facts about the CIA and the FBI  and the IRS and the NSA came out a  couple of those initials may sound  familiar from recent stories so this is  not something it only happens in the  1970s but even when a conspiracy story  is not accurate at all it says something  true about the anxieties and the  experiences of the people who believe  and repeat it even if it says nothing  true about the objects of the theory  itself and when people try to talk about  political paranoia while ignoring those  facts I think you get really  one-dimensional discussions of the  subject and my book I set out to get  past that I hope I got at least a bit  pest it in my talk here today
 

